※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A reference is only worth having while what it names is still there, and a
※ caller cannot see into the function to work that out.  So a function handing
※ one back says how long that is, and there are two answers: `static` in the
※ type says "as long as the program", and a lifetime name written after `⧖` says
※ "as long as whatever else in this signature carries the same name".
※
※ **Both stand in one slot**, after `mut` and before what is pointed at, since
※ they answer one question: `&mut static u8` and `&mut ⧖x u8`.  At most one of
※ them is ever written, and writing neither is refused (4562) -- there is no
※ default, the two meaning different things to a caller.
※
※ **The caller works out the rest.**  A function promises no more than its
※ parameters' lifetime, so `first(&total)` -- where `total` is a variable at the
※ top level -- answers with a reference that lasts as long as the program, while
※ `first(&n)` for a local answers with one that does not.  The same function,
※ two answers, decided where both are in view.
※
※ Rust writes both of these as named lifetime parameters, `fn first<'a>(v: &'a
※ u8) -> &'a u8` and `&'static u8`, declared at the head of the signature.  Here
※ a lifetime is declared by being used, as a type parameter is, so there is no
※ list to write and none to keep in step.  C++ has no such rule and a dangling
※ reference is a program nobody notices is wrong; Go and Java move what escapes
※ to the heap instead, which needs a collector.

let total: mut u8 = 0u8
let other: mut u8 = 0u8

※ As long as the program: what it names is a variable at the top level, which is
※ there before the first call and after the last.
fn counter() → &mut static u8:
    &mut total

※ As long as what `v` named, which is all this can promise and all a caller
※ needs: it hands back what it was given.
fn first(v: &⧖a u8) → &⧖a u8:
    v

※ Which of several parameters, out of those that carry the name.
fn second(p: &u8, q: &⧖b u8) → &⧖b u8:
    q

※ A reference handed on through another call keeps the promise it was made
※ with, so the two signatures agree with nothing written between them.
fn again(v: &⧖c u8) → &⧖c u8:
    first(v)

@[impure]
fn bump(at: &mut ⧖d u8) → &mut ⧖d u8:
    at⌖ ← at⌖ + 1u8
    at

※ A promise about the program's whole life is kept by anything that lasts that
※ long, so answering with a variable at the top level is allowed.
fn ignoring(v: &⧖e u8) → &⧖e u8:
    &other

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ a reference that lasts as long as the program, written through
    let c: &mut static u8 = counter()
    c⌖ ← 5u8
    ※ and standing where one with less to promise is wanted, which it may
    let shorter: &mut u8 = counter()
    shorter⌖ ← shorter⌖ + 1u8
    ※ borrowed from a local, which lives as long as the body
    let n: u8 = 7u8
    let r: &u8 = first(&n)
    let again_n: u8 = 7u8
    let again_r: &u8 = again(&again_n)
    ※ borrowed from the one of two that carries the name
    let x: u8 = 1u8
    let y: u8 = 2u8
    let q: &u8 = second(&x, &y)
    ※ the same function called with a top-level variable answers with one that
    ※ lasts as long as the program, which is what the type here demands
    let lasting: &static u8 = first(&total)
    ※ handed on and written through
    let m: mut u8 = 40u8   ※ named no more: `b` is the only way to it now
    let b: &mut u8 = bump(&mut m)
    b⌖ ← b⌖ + 1u8
    let told: u8 = 3u8
    if total ≠ 6u8:
        1u6
    elif r⌖ ≠ 7u8 ∨ again_r⌖ ≠ 7u8:
        2u6
    elif q⌖ ≠ 2u8:
        3u6
    elif lasting⌖ ≠ 6u8:
        4u6
    elif b⌖ ≠ 42u8:
        5u6
    elif ignoring(&told)⌖ ≠ 0u8:
        6u6
    else:
        0u6
